// spec §11.1(4): "Rules (2) and (3) do not otherwise restrict a `struct`.
// A `struct` may declare ... non-static data members ... exactly as the
// ordinary C++ rules permit." §6.1(5) then *regulates* the construct --
// "a constructor for a class or struct with a reference member is
// ill-formed unless that member is initialized" -- a rule that would have
// nothing to govern if a struct could not have one. So a reference-typed
// struct field is well-formed and lowers exactly like a class's: a
// pointer member, stored as the address of the referent.
struct RefBox { int& r; };
int main() {
    int x = 1;
    RefBox b{x};
    b.r = 9;
    return x - 9;
}
